## Resolution · engineering update, 5 October 2026

The course's Challenge page now reports **#4471 closed 05 Oct**:

- **Parser — fixed:** `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. The parser ticket is closed as completed.
- **Hint — won't fix:** booking-v3 form retired. The hint ticket is closed as not planned, not as a shipped hint fix.

These outcomes come from the engineering update on the [Challenge page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows),
not from production tests we ran. Counts below remain the export snapshot through 4 October.

## Decision for the desk

Do not treat “known” as “one cause” or “resolved.” Split the merged date reports by tenant
locale, booking-form version, and channel. The parser fix did not itself fix the misleading hint;
retiring booking-v3 removed that form instead.
This reassessment supersedes the one-cause conclusion in [the original incident](/kb/incidents/4471).

<IncidentEvidence />

## What the rendered evidence shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} issueLinks={{ parser: 'https://github.com/JanuszJakubiec/megadev-s01e03/issues/1', hint: 'https://github.com/JanuszJakubiec/megadev-s01e03/issues/2' }} />

### Shared parser: month first for day-first tenants

The shared parser treats dates as MM/DD while the affected tenants write DD/MM. App and email
reports, and booking-v2 form reports, belong here. For example, R-41200 reports a 7 April reminder
sent for 4 July. Use an explicit unambiguous input contract and tenant-aware parsing; test
ambiguous dates, invalid month/day values, and app/email/form paths before marking fixed.

### Booking-v3: wrong date hint

Booking-v3 prints MM/DD/YYYY even for a day-first tenant, while the form reads dates in that
tenant's order. People following the hint get a different booking date. This affects form reports
from alder.physio, copper.studio, and pale.harbour, not every report from those tenants.
R-41230 and R-41241 illustrate this path. Make the hint agree with the tenant's actual input
format and validate with an end-to-end submission test. Keep this ticket separate from the parser.

### What not to infer

Browser locale and intake tags are guesses, not tenant configuration. Subject text is not a
cause key. The five reports listed in desk-notes.json describe a one-day move, but the supplied
tenant-based logic files them under parser; the cause table footnotes them there. Do not count
them twice or invent a confirmed timezone bug. Preserve their IDs for follow-up on that symptom.

## The desk kept working

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The received and merged series measure different events. A batch of merges is not a burst of
new failures. Reports continued to arrive after the initial merge batch through the export cutoff: “known” did not stop
customer impact. The later engineering update closes the incident with different outcomes for each cause.
For future reports, re-run the grouping for new exports, update each ticket's unique report count,
and route by cause instead of folding them into the biggest ticket. A new failure after closure
needs fresh investigation, not an assumption that both causes received a code fix.

## Triage rule going forward

[Decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) replaces accepted decision 0001 without
rewriting its history. Compare tenant locale, form and channel, then read the report for
contradictions. Same subject can hide different causes; different subjects can describe the same
cause. Unknown tenants remain unplaced pending investigation, never automatically duplicates.

## Reproduce and maintain

Run `npm ci`, `npm test`, `node scripts/analyze.mjs`, `npm run check`, and `npm run build`.
The built page is `build/kb/incidents/4471-reassessment/index.html`; serve `build/` to see it
with its CSS and JavaScript. Counts are calculated by components from the export. The build
also renders the original pages, so prose can be checked against their live tables and charts.
The page's desk reference below is computed by RenderStamp from its frontmatter signature.

